1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,020
This is a smartphone
you can buy today. 5G.

2
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:05,920
A recent version of Android.

3
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:11,120
Twelve gigabytes of memory, five
hundred and twelve gigabytes of storage.

4
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:14,150
And a physical QWERTY keyboard. Real keys.

5
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:16,130
Ones you actually press.

6
00:00:16,845 --> 00:00:21,085
Not a ten-year-old BlackBerry
from eBay. A new phone.

7
00:00:21,871 --> 00:00:24,891
Touchscreens won this fight
more than fifteen years ago.

8
00:00:25,491 --> 00:00:27,531
So why does this thing even exist?

9
00:00:27,884 --> 00:00:31,884
And who would want one? There's a
rather unexpected answer to that.

10
00:00:31,964 --> 00:00:33,164
But we'll get there.

11
00:00:35,494 --> 00:00:38,414
To understand it, we need
to go back to BlackBerry.

12
00:00:39,131 --> 00:00:41,951
BlackBerry didn't invent
the keyboard on a phone.

13
00:00:42,611 --> 00:00:44,931
But it turned the idea into a phenomenon.

14
00:00:45,651 --> 00:00:49,711
Tiny screen, tiny buttons, and a
full QWERTY keyboard underneath.

15
00:00:50,531 --> 00:00:53,111
The phone felt almost
like a tiny computer.

16
00:00:53,877 --> 00:00:57,177
You could type without covering
half the screen with your fingers.

17
00:00:57,957 --> 00:01:00,737
For people who lived in
email, that was a big deal.

18
00:01:01,356 --> 00:01:05,316
BlackBerry became the phone of
business, messaging and productivity.

19
00:01:06,631 --> 00:01:08,531
Then the iPhone came along.

20
00:01:08,611 --> 00:01:12,711
And the touchscreen allowed one
device to become almost anything.

21
00:01:13,470 --> 00:01:16,270
The keyboard didn't have
to be permanently attached.

22
00:01:16,990 --> 00:01:20,470
It could change language.
It could change layout.

23
00:01:20,870 --> 00:01:23,670
It could disappear completely
when you didn't need it.

24
00:01:23,815 --> 00:01:28,235
And most importantly, the entire front
of the phone could become a screen.

25
00:01:28,625 --> 00:01:31,505
So the physical keyboard
gradually disappeared.

26
00:01:32,145 --> 00:01:35,505
BlackBerry itself eventually
stopped making its own phones.

27
00:01:36,253 --> 00:01:38,953
A company that later tried
to revive the brand with

28
00:01:39,183 --> 00:01:42,553
a new keyboard phone never
actually released one.

29
00:01:43,112 --> 00:01:48,192
By the early 2020s, it really
did look like the story was over.

30
00:01:49,176 --> 00:01:51,936
Except... while Apple,
Samsung and everyone else were

31
00:01:52,166 --> 00:01:54,856
making increasingly
similar glass rectangles...

32
00:01:55,437 --> 00:01:59,157
a few small companies were doing
something completely different.

33
00:01:59,885 --> 00:02:01,465
One of them is the small smartphone

34
00:02:01,695 --> 00:02:04,745
manufacturer Unihertz,
with its Titan series.

35
00:02:05,545 --> 00:02:08,045
It's been making them since 2019.

36
00:02:08,794 --> 00:02:11,094
The idea is remarkably simple.

37
00:02:11,754 --> 00:02:12,794
Take an Android smartphone...

38
00:02:13,024 --> 00:02:16,134
and put a physical keyboard
underneath the screen.

39
00:02:16,743 --> 00:02:22,123
The Titan 2 is the phone from the start
of this video. Not a Bluetooth keyboard.

40
00:02:22,203 --> 00:02:25,623
Not an accessory. The keys
are part of the phone.

41
00:02:25,915 --> 00:02:28,115
And the newer Titan 2
Elite comes with Android

42
00:02:28,345 --> 00:02:32,035
16 and a hundred and twenty
hertz AMOLED display.

43
00:02:32,773 --> 00:02:35,533
The keyboard can even act
as a kind of touchpad.

44
00:02:35,913 --> 00:02:40,793
You can move the cursor, scroll through
pages, and assign shortcuts to keys.

45
00:02:41,333 --> 00:02:44,353
So this isn't an old BlackBerry
brought back to life.

46
00:02:44,813 --> 00:02:46,213
It's a modern Android phone...

47
00:02:46,443 --> 00:02:48,573
that happens to have a keyboard.

48
00:02:49,788 --> 00:02:51,608
And Unihertz isn't alone.

49
00:02:52,444 --> 00:02:55,084
Planet Computers made the Astro Slide.

50
00:02:55,784 --> 00:02:59,504
Its screen slid upward and revealed
a mechanical keyboard underneath.

51
00:03:00,307 --> 00:03:04,087
It was much closer to a tiny computer
than to a traditional smartphone.

52
00:03:04,987 --> 00:03:08,067
The keyboard was backlit,
programmable, and came in many

53
00:03:08,297 --> 00:03:11,587
language layouts, including
Czech, Swedish and Danish.

54
00:03:12,331 --> 00:03:13,306
Then there was F(x)tec.

55
00:03:13,536 --> 00:03:17,051
Their Pro1 X looked like an
ordinary touchscreen phone...

56
00:03:17,281 --> 00:03:20,451
until it slid open to
reveal a full keyboard.

57
00:03:21,195 --> 00:03:25,355
It came out in 2022, so it's
hardly a current flagship.

58
00:03:25,735 --> 00:03:29,595
But it shows the idea was never
limited to a single company.

59
00:03:29,831 --> 00:03:33,631
And new projects keep appearing,
such as the Clicks Communicator.

60
00:03:34,122 --> 00:03:38,822
So there is clearly still a market
for this idea. Tiny... but real.

61
00:03:39,932 --> 00:03:43,412
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

62
00:03:44,032 --> 00:03:45,652
It really helps the channel.

63
00:03:48,222 --> 00:03:49,242
But why?

64
00:03:49,322 --> 00:03:53,262
Because there is something a touchscreen
keyboard can't completely reproduce.

65
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:55,960
You can feel a physical key.

66
00:03:56,515 --> 00:04:00,375
When you type on glass, your finger
doesn't know where the next letter is.

67
00:04:00,895 --> 00:04:02,095
You have to look.

68
00:04:02,650 --> 00:04:05,670
With physical keys, your
fingers find their position.

69
00:04:06,350 --> 00:04:08,110
You feel the gap between keys.

70
00:04:08,590 --> 00:04:10,250
You feel when a key was pressed.

71
00:04:10,585 --> 00:04:12,505
And once you've learned
the layout, you can

72
00:04:12,735 --> 00:04:15,285
sometimes type while
looking at something else.

73
00:04:15,905 --> 00:04:18,685
That's a completely different
way of using a phone.

74
00:04:19,787 --> 00:04:23,967
It's the same reason physical keyboards
never disappeared from computers.

75
00:04:24,789 --> 00:04:29,709
Nobody asks, "Why do programmers still use
keyboards? We already have touchscreens."

76
00:04:30,567 --> 00:04:35,367
Because sometimes physical controls
are simply better. Not for everything.

77
00:04:35,447 --> 00:04:37,207
But for some things.

78
00:04:38,163 --> 00:04:41,323
And on a modern phone, a keyboard
doesn't just mean typing.

79
00:04:42,063 --> 00:04:43,763
The keys can work as shortcuts.

80
00:04:44,768 --> 00:04:46,748
Press one key to open an app.

81
00:04:46,848 --> 00:04:48,928
Hold another to run an action.

82
00:04:49,628 --> 00:04:51,588
Swipe across the keys to scroll.

83
00:04:52,113 --> 00:04:53,813
So the keyboard becomes another input

84
00:04:54,043 --> 00:04:56,993
device, not just a way
of entering letters.

85
00:04:57,777 --> 00:05:00,517
These phones aren't trying
to recreate the past.

86
00:05:01,217 --> 00:05:04,697
They combine an old interface
with a modern operating system.

87
00:05:07,177 --> 00:05:09,937
So if it's that useful, why is it so rare?

88
00:05:10,980 --> 00:05:12,780
The first problem is space.

89
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:16,120
Every millimetre of a
smartphone is valuable.

90
00:05:16,923 --> 00:05:21,763
Put a keyboard under the screen, and you
need a bigger phone. Or a smaller screen.

91
00:05:22,143 --> 00:05:23,343
Or both.

92
00:05:23,679 --> 00:05:26,059
That's a hard sell when
everyone else is competing to

93
00:05:26,289 --> 00:05:29,459
make phones thinner, lighter,
and almost entirely screen.

94
00:05:30,981 --> 00:05:32,961
The second problem is language.

95
00:05:33,641 --> 00:05:36,781
A physical keyboard needs a
different layout for each one.

96
00:05:38,268 --> 00:05:40,508
QWERTY. QWERTZ. AZERTY.
Cyrillic. And many others.

97
00:05:41,180 --> 00:05:42,960
A touchscreen doesn't care.

98
00:05:43,500 --> 00:05:45,900
Change the software, and
you've changed the keyboard.

99
00:05:46,739 --> 00:05:50,059
With physical keys, you have to
manufacture something different.

100
00:05:50,139 --> 00:05:53,379
And suddenly your market
gets much smaller.

101
00:05:54,448 --> 00:05:56,888
So it's not that the
technology doesn't work.

102
00:05:57,268 --> 00:06:00,588
It works perfectly well.
The problem is economics.

103
00:06:03,198 --> 00:06:05,598
Millions of people want a large screen.

104
00:06:06,118 --> 00:06:07,878
Millions want a good camera.

105
00:06:08,498 --> 00:06:10,218
Millions want a thin phone.

106
00:06:10,407 --> 00:06:15,047
But how many people specifically
want a physical keyboard? Far fewer.

107
00:06:15,817 --> 00:06:18,657
That's why Samsung doesn't
make a phone like this.

108
00:06:19,357 --> 00:06:20,877
Apple doesn't make one.

109
00:06:21,237 --> 00:06:22,957
Google doesn't make one.

110
00:06:23,191 --> 00:06:25,311
Instead, you get small
companies making unusual

111
00:06:25,541 --> 00:06:28,371
phones for people who
want something different.

112
00:06:28,491 --> 00:06:30,551
And perhaps that's enough.

113
00:06:33,241 --> 00:06:37,481
So is the physical keyboard
really dead? Well... not quite.

114
00:06:38,267 --> 00:06:42,607
It's no longer mainstream. You
won't find it in a phone shop.

115
00:06:42,687 --> 00:06:46,467
And you're certainly not going to
see a new BlackBerry on the shelf.

116
00:06:46,756 --> 00:06:50,596
But the idea didn't
disappear. It became a niche.

117
00:06:51,296 --> 00:06:54,456
And sometimes that's exactly
how technologies survive.

118
00:06:55,304 --> 00:06:58,844
They don't have to win. They just
have to remain useful to somebody.

119
00:07:02,094 --> 00:07:04,674
And that unexpected answer from the start?

120
00:07:05,833 --> 00:07:10,513
This script was written on a phone
with a physical keyboard. This one.

121
00:07:11,943 --> 00:07:15,443
And so were the scripts for a lot
of other videos on this channel.

122
00:07:16,071 --> 00:07:18,531
The physical keyboard didn't really die.

123
00:07:19,111 --> 00:07:21,671
It just became something
you have to look for.

124
00:07:22,070 --> 00:07:25,040
And if you really want one,
you can still buy one today.
